<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">{{ site.researchByUrl[page.url].kicker }}</p>
  <h1>Temporal Antipodes</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">The smaller infinity and the bigger infinity cannot ever fully synchronise because
    they exist at opposite ends of time.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The conjecture in one sentence</p>
    <h2 id="t-proposal">They Are Not Two Clocks That Happen to Disagree</h2>
    <p class="lede">They are proposed boundary conditions at opposite temporal asymptotes. Complete
      synchronisation would require a shared finite instant, a comparison rule and a channel that can carry
      timing information between them. On this conjecture, no such common clock exists.</p>
    <p>That conclusion is conditional, not a theorem of Cantor, relativity or thermodynamics. It follows only
      if the two scale-directions are genuinely assigned to opposite ends of time, those ends remain
      asymptotic rather than physically identified, and synchronisation is required to be an operational
      relation rather than a resemblance in a diagram. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established mathematics or physics</span>
      &middot; <span class="spec">&#9670; TTI conjecture</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; philosophical image</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">First keep three meanings apart</p>
    <h2 id="t-names">Smallward Is Not Mathematically Smaller</h2>
    <p><strong>Smallward infinity</strong> means the direction of scale toward zero: halve a positive
      magnitude without end and it approaches a boundary it never reaches. <strong>Largeward infinity</strong>
      means the direction through larger and larger magnitudes. These are geometric directions, not claims
      about the cardinal sizes of sets.</p>
    <p>Indeed, the established mathematics points the other way if the comparison is the real unit interval
      against the natural numbers:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \left|\mathbb R\cap(0,1)\right|=2^{\aleph_0}
      &gt;
      \aleph_0=\left|\mathbb N\right|.
    \]</p>
    <p>The interval of numerically small values is uncountable, while the outward integer sequence is
      countable. The words <em>smaller</em> and <em>bigger</em> therefore name the magnitudes approached, not
      the amount of infinity present. Assigning either direction to an end of time is a further TTI postulate;
      Cantor&rsquo;s result does not do it. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span>
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

